+---
+title: "A TObject's fBits/fUniqueID the file holds are not what TTree::Show prints"
+kind: "gotcha"
+summary: "ROOT prints fBits with kIsOnHeap|kNotDeleted and fUniqueID with the process in its top byte, and both are set on the object, not by the record reader - so fix them where objects are made, never in buffer.py."
+tags: [tobject, fbits, funiqueid, references, interp, roottest, io-tree]
+created: "2026-11-21T06:32:55.404Z"
+updated: "2026-11-21T06:32:55.404Z"
+---
+`TTree::Show`, `TScan` and a `TRef` all report two numbers a ROOT file does not hold: an object's `fBits` with `kIsOnHeap|kNotDeleted` (0x03000000) set, and its `fUniqueID` with the process in the top byte. Both are put there by ROOT's *reader when it fills an object*, so the fix belongs where an object is made - not in the byte reader, and not in the split-branch column reader unless the column is the only place the value exists.
+
+## What ROOT actually stores and prints
+
+Measured with the 6.40.04 oracle (`~/.local/root-oracle/bin/root -l -b -q probe.C`, `probe2.C`, `show.C` in `/tmp/r30/oracle`) against `roottest/root/io/tree`'s `tref_test_pid*.root`, which are written by `create`/`addref` in `/tmp/rootsrc-6.40b/roottest/root/io/tree/tref_test_pid.C`:
+
+- the file holds `fBits & ~0x03000000` (`wdobjects.py` masks on write, correctly) and `Show` prints the file's word with those two bits back: stored `0x18` prints `50331672`, stored `0` prints `50331648`;
+- the file holds a plain number (`09 00 00 00 08 …` in the `N.fUniqueID` basket) and `Show` prints `16777225` = `0x01000009` for it. The top byte is the **file's `ProcessID` index + 1**, not the index: `test-rc` (only `ProcessID0` in the file) prints `0x01…`, `test-rcr` (whose `r1` objects belong to `ProcessID1`) prints `0x02…`. The +1 is `TBufferIO::GetPidOffset`; `TKey` keeps that offset in the high bits of `fSeekPdir`. A probe that read an unsplit pidf-0 object in a session holding two processes got `0x00000000`, so it is a property of the key, not a constant.
+
+## Why not in `buffer.py::referenced`
+
+`Buffer.referenced()` is the `TObject` word reader under every read path. Returning `bits | 0x03000000` there was tried and broke seven tests that pin the file's word, because these members are read and written again as they came in: `tests/test_wdobjects_nested_records.py`, `test_histfactory_streamed.py`, `test_cli_diff.py`, `test_root_known.py`, `test_roofit_io_records.py`, `test_interp_member_wise_bases.py`, `test_root_containers.py`. The process likewise stays beside the identifier (`referenced()` returns `(unique, bits, pidf)`) so a `TRef`/`TRefArray` record written back is byte-identical. The object-side rule already lives in `pyroot/core/objects.py` (`TObject._bitword` adds `HEAP_BITS`, `_store_bits` drops it) and in `pyroot/core/declared.py::root_row`, which masks it again on the way out.
+
+## Where the io/tree family really needs it
+
+`roottest/root/io/tree/test-rc{,-rcr,-rcr2,-rcrr,-rcrr2,-rcrr3}` print through `TTree::Show`, which reads **split-branch** values (`pyroot/trees/player.py`, `Show` → `self._batch.value(leaf.column, at)`); `N.fUniqueID`, `N.fBits` and `r1.fBits` are `interp.Rows` columns (`src/xrdroot/interp.py`, `build` → `_element` → `_primitive_element` → `_primitive_shape`) that read the basket bytes directly, and for a split collection the pidf is nowhere in the file. The reassembly therefore has to happen in `interp.py`'s element read (the process from the basket key's pid offset, the bits always), and an object built from those columns (`pyroot/trees/rebuild.py`, `pyroot/core/wrapping.py::from_members`) needs the same information from whoever holds the file.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  F[file bytes: fUniqueID=9, fBits=0x18, no pidf in split branches] --> R[Buffer.referenced / interp Rows]
+  R -->|"as written, always"| W[written back byte-identical]
+  R -->|"interp element read: +bits, +process byte"| S[TTree::Show / Scan]
+  R --> M[object made: TObject._bitword adds HEAP_BITS, declared.root_row masks]
+  M --> P[printed fBits / GetUniqueID / TRef GetObject]
+```
